<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarfaya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Villa Bens</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tarfaya is a small town on the Atlantic coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-juby">Cape Juby</a>. Under the name Villa Bens it was the centre of the southern zone of the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, the Tarfaya Strip between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draa-river">Draa River</a> and the 27th parallel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> transferred the strip to Morocco by the Agreement of Angra de Cintra in April 1958, two years after the northern zone had joined independent Morocco.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarfaya.jpg" alt="Tarfaya" width="320" height="122" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ElWaliElAlaoui · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarfaya_city.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
